__d(function(e,a,t,i,r,n,s){r.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 History",textbook:"Our Pasts-I",chapter_number:1,chapter_title:"What, Where, How and When?",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the three main sources historians use to find out about the past?",back:"The three main sources are: (1) Manuscripts \u2014 handwritten books on palm leaf or birch bark; (2) Inscriptions \u2014 writing carved on stone or metal; (3) Archaeological remains \u2014 objects, buildings, and tools excavated from the earth. Together, these three sources help build a complete picture of the past.",memory_hook:"MIA = Manuscripts, Inscriptions, Archaeology. MIA are the three detectives history uses to solve the mystery of the past!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a manuscript? On what material were they written?",back:"A manuscript is a handwritten book containing religious texts, poems, plays, epics, or scientific knowledge. In ancient India, manuscripts were written on palm leaf or birch bark. They were usually preserved in temples and monasteries. The word 'manuscript' comes from the Latin 'manu' (hand) + 'scriptus' (written).",memory_hook:"MANUSCRIPT = Made And Noted by hand, Usually Stored by Candle in Religious Places Today. Palm leaf + hand writing = manuscript!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an inscription? How is it different from a manuscript?",back:"An inscription is writing carved or engraved on a hard surface \u2014 stone pillars, temple walls, copper plates, or bricks. Unlike manuscripts (which decay), inscriptions are extremely durable and can last thousands of years. They often record royal orders, land grants, or religious dedications. Example: Ashoka's stone pillar edicts, Halmidi inscription (Karnataka).",memory_hook:"INSCRIPTION = Carved IN STONE = Can't be destroyed easily. Manuscripts fade; inscriptions stay. Think of a gravestone \u2014 it lasts centuries!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does BCE stand for, and how do BCE dates work?",back:"BCE stands for Before Common Era. BCE dates count backwards from the year 0 \u2014 the larger the BCE number, the further back in time. For example, 3000 BCE is much earlier (further in the past) than 500 BCE. The Harappan civilisation began around 2500 BCE \u2014 about 4500+ years ago. BCE was previously called 'BC'.",memory_hook:"BCE = Before Count Entered positive territory. Think of a rocket countdown: 5-4-3-2-1. The higher the count, the further from launch (year 0). 3000 BCE is like being at '3000' on the countdown!"},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Along which river did the earliest skilled gatherers of the subcontinent live?",back:"The earliest skilled gatherers lived along the banks of the Narmada river. People have lived there for several hundred thousand years. They gathered roots, fruits, and forest produce for food, and also hunted animals. They had deep knowledge of the plants and forests around them.",memory_hook:"NARMADA = Naturally Abundant Resources Made Ancient Dwellers Active. The Narmada valley was the first cafeteria of ancient India \u2014 full of food to gather!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Where were the earliest cities of the subcontinent built, and approximately when?",back:"The earliest cities of the Indian subcontinent were built on the banks of the Indus river and its tributaries \u2014 approximately 4700 years ago (around 2700 BCE). These cities include Harappa and Mohenjo-daro (in present-day Pakistan). They were remarkably well-planned with drainage systems, granaries, and standardised bricks.",memory_hook:"INDUS = India's New and Distinguished Urban Story. The Indus cities were India's first cities \u2014 planned, drained, and brilliant, 4700 years ago!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a tributary? Give one example.",back:"A tributary is a smaller river that flows into a larger river. The point where a tributary meets the main river is called a confluence. Example: The Yamuna is a tributary of the Ganga. The earliest cities were built along the Indus and its tributaries \u2014 smaller rivers that flow into the main Indus river.",memory_hook:"TRIBUTARY = 'pays tribute' (flows into) a bigger river. A tributary is like a student contributing to a class \u2014 it joins the bigger group (main river)!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'subcontinent' mean? Which countries make up the Indian subcontinent?",back:"A subcontinent is a large landmass that is part of a continent but geographically distinct from the rest of it. The Indian subcontinent is separated from the rest of Asia by the Himalaya mountains and includes: India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, and Sri Lanka.",memory_hook:"SUB-continent = a BIG piece UNDER the larger continent. Think of India as a huge pendant hanging from the 'necklace' of Asia \u2014 distinct, large, and geographically separated!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name three different names that have been used for the Indian subcontinent.",back:"Three names used for the Indian subcontinent: (1) Bharata \u2014 an ancient name derived from a legendary king, still used today (India's official name in the Constitution is 'Bharat'); (2) India \u2014 used by the Greeks and Persians, derived from the Indus/Sindhu river; (3) Hindustan \u2014 used by Central Asian travellers and invaders, meaning 'land of the Hindus/Sindhu'.",memory_hook:"BIH = Bharata, India, Hindustan = Three names, one great land! Ancient kings called it Bharata, outsiders called it India or Hindustan \u2014 all the same place!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What do historians mean when they say 'there were many pasts, not just one'?",back:"This means that different groups of people \u2014 rulers, farmers, hunters, merchants, women, crafts persons, children \u2014 all lived through the same historical period but had completely different experiences. A king's 'past' is full of politics and battles. A farmer's 'past' is about crops and seasons. History is richest when it includes all these different stories, not just the story of kings.",memory_hook:"MANY PASTS = Many People's Stories. Use FARM-R: Farmers, Artists, Rulers, Merchants, Rest of society \u2014 all had different pasts in the same time period!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why is it important that archaeologists excavate carefully and systematically rather than just digging randomly?",back:"Careful, systematic excavation preserves the 'context' of each find \u2014 what object was found near what, at what depth, in what layer of soil. This context is critical because it tells archaeologists when the object was deposited (deeper layers are usually older), how it was used, and what it was associated with. Random digging destroys this context forever, making the objects far less useful for understanding the past.",memory_hook:"CONTEXT = the story behind the object. An ancient coin found near a marketplace ruin means trade. The same coin found alone with no context tells much less. Context = location + association + depth = the full story!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why do historians use all three sources (manuscripts, inscriptions, and archaeology) rather than relying on just one?",back:"Each source has strengths and limitations. Manuscripts give ideas, language, and religious/literary knowledge \u2014 but they decay. Inscriptions give durable records of royal commands and dates \u2014 but they only record what rulers wanted to say. Archaeology gives material evidence of everyday life (tools, pottery, buildings) \u2014 but objects cannot 'speak'. Together, all three sources provide a more complete, balanced, and cross-checked picture of the past.",memory_hook:"Three sources = Three Witnesses in court. One witness can be wrong. Three witnesses who agree give you confidence in the truth! Manuscripts + Inscriptions + Archaeology = History's three witnesses."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Where did the earliest agriculture begin in the Indian subcontinent? What crops were grown?",back:"The earliest agriculture in the Indian subcontinent began in the Sulaiman and Kirthar hills (in the northwest, present-day Pakistan and Afghanistan) about 8000 years ago. Women and men first grew wheat and barley here. People also began rearing animals \u2014 sheep, goats, and cattle. Agriculture also developed in the Garo hills (northeast) and the Vindhyas (central India), where rice was first cultivated to the north of the Vindhyas.",memory_hook:"S-K-G-V = Sulaiman-Kirthar (wheat/barley), Garo hills, Vindhyas (rice) = India's first farmers' geography. SKY + V = the four corners of India's agricultural revolution!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a primary source and a secondary source in history?",back:"A primary source is created at the time of the event being studied \u2014 by people who were there. Manuscripts, inscriptions, coins, and archaeological remains are all primary sources. A secondary source is created later, using primary sources \u2014 like a history textbook or a biography. Historians prefer primary sources because they are closer to the original events, though they must still be critically examined.",memory_hook:"PRIMARY = First-hand. SECONDARY = Second-hand. If you read a diary from 1947 = primary. If you read a book about 1947 written in 2000 = secondary. Primary is the original; secondary is a copy or retelling!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why is the Narmada river so significant in the early history of the Indian subcontinent?",back:"The Narmada valley is one of the earliest areas of human habitation on the Indian subcontinent. People have lived along the Narmada for several hundred thousand years. The earliest inhabitants were skilled gatherers who had deep knowledge of forest plants and animals. The region's rich forests, reliable water supply, and diverse wildlife made it an ideal location for early human settlement. The Narmada thus represents the very beginning of human history in India.",memory_hook:"NARMADA = India's First Home. If Indian history were a house, the Narmada valley is the foundation \u2014 the oldest inhabited area, built several hundred thousand years ago!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Where did people later settle in the Ganga region, and why was the Ganga important?",back:"People later settled along the Ganga and its tributaries in the fertile plains of north India. The Ganga plains became the heartland of many later kingdoms and republics (from about 2500 years ago). The river provided water for irrigation, supported dense agriculture, and enabled river-based trade. Many of the major kingdoms and cities of ancient India \u2014 including Pataliputra (modern Patna) \u2014 were located along the Ganga.",memory_hook:"GANGA = Great Ancient Networks of Growing Alliances. The Ganga was ancient India's highway \u2014 for water, trade, and kingdoms. From gathering (Narmada) to farming (Indus hills) to kingdoms (Ganga) = India's history moves eastward!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"If you found an old copper plate with writing on it in your village, would it be a manuscript or an inscription? What information might it contain?",back:"A copper plate with writing carved on it would be an inscription \u2014 specifically a copper plate inscription. This was a common way for rulers to record land grants (donating land to priests or temples), royal orders, or legal decisions. It might contain the name of the king who issued it, the date, the land being granted, the recipient, and the witnesses. Copper plate inscriptions have been found across Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, and Andhra Pradesh and are very important historical sources.",memory_hook:"Copper plate = Inscription (carved/etched into metal). If it's WRITTEN BY HAND on papyrus/palm leaf = manuscript. If it's CARVED/ETCHED into a hard surface (stone, copper, brick) = inscription. The material and method of writing are the key!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"A date is given as 1500 BCE. How many years ago from today (2024 CE) would that be?",back:"To calculate years from a BCE date to today: Add the BCE date + the current CE year. So 1500 BCE + 2024 CE = 3524 years ago. This is because BCE dates count backwards from year 0, and CE dates count forwards. So 1500 BCE + 2024 CE = 3524 years total between that date and today.",memory_hook:"BCE to Today = BCE number + CE year. 1500 BCE to 2024 CE = 1500 + 2024 = 3524 years ago. Think of it as jumping from -1500 to +2024 on a number line: the total distance is 1500 + 2024 = 3524!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"The chapter says history is about 'what people ate, wore, houses they lived in, games children played, songs they sang.' Why does the NCERT include these 'everyday' details in a history chapter?",back:"The NCERT includes everyday details deliberately to challenge the idea that history is only about wars and kings. By mentioning food, clothing, houses, children's games, and songs, the textbook shows that history is about the full human experience \u2014 not just power and politics. This also makes history more relatable and exciting for students. It means everyone's life is historically significant, not just those who held power. This approach reflects modern historical methods that include social history, cultural history, and women's history.",memory_hook:"EVERYDAY HISTORY = YOUR history too! What you eat for breakfast, the games you play, the songs you hum \u2014 future historians might study these to understand YOUR time. History is the story of ALL human life, not just battles!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Why does the chapter title ask 'What, Where, How and When?' specifically? What is the significance of these four questions?",back:"These four questions are the foundation of historical inquiry. WHAT: What happened \u2014 what events, what discoveries, what changes occurred? WHERE: Where did these happen \u2014 which river valleys, cities, regions? HOW: How do we know \u2014 what sources, what methods did historians use? WHEN: When did these occur \u2014 what are the BCE/CE dates? Every chapter in Our Pasts-I answers these four questions. By starting with them, the textbook teaches students to think like historians \u2014 to always ask what, where, how, and when before accepting any historical claim.",memory_hook:"WHAT WHERE HOW WHEN = The Four Historian's Questions = W-W-H-W = 'Will We Have Wisdom?' A historian without these four questions is like a detective without clues \u2014 lost!"}]}},2911,[]);